Kept the list links in place here. A dummy node gives every group a node before it, so the first group does not need special handling. For each group, I find the kth node first. If it is missing, the remaining nodes are already in the required order.

For example, `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` with `k = 2` becomes `2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 5`. The last node is not touched.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseKGroup(ListNode* head, int k) {
        if (head == nullptr || k <= 1) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode dummy(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        ListNode* groupPrev = &dummy;

        while (true) {
            ListNode* kth = groupPrev;
            for (int i = 0; i < k && kth != nullptr; ++i) {
                kth = kth->next;
            }

            if (kth == nullptr) {
                break;
            }

            ListNode* groupStart = groupPrev->next;
            ListNode* groupNext = kth->next;

            ListNode* previous = groupNext;
            ListNode* current = groupStart;

            while (current != groupNext) {
                ListNode* nextNode = current->next;
                current->next = previous;
                previous = current;
                current = nextNode;
            }

            groupPrev->next = kth;
            groupPrev = groupStart;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each node is checked and reversed a constant number of times.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input list is changed in place.

I used the normal linked-list reverse function, but only after cutting out a complete group. First, the code finds the last node of the group and saves the next group. Then it disconnects the group, reverses it, and joins both sides again.

### Dry run
With `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6` and `k = 3`, the first detached part is `1 -> 2 -> 3`. It becomes `3 -> 2 -> 1`, then connects to `4 -> 5 -> 6`. The second group is handled the same way.

The code also handles a null head, `k <= 1`, and a final group that is too short. The input links are modified directly.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* previous = nullptr;
        ListNode* current = head;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            ListNode* nextNode = current->next;
            current->next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return previous;
    }

public:
    ListNode* reverseKGroup(ListNode* head, int k) {
        if (head == nullptr || k <= 1) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode* answer = head;
        ListNode* previousTail = nullptr;
        ListNode* groupStart = head;

        while (groupStart != nullptr) {
            ListNode* groupEnd = groupStart;

            for (int i = 1; i < k && groupEnd != nullptr; i++) {
                groupEnd = groupEnd->next;
            }

            if (groupEnd == nullptr) {
                if (previousTail != nullptr) {
                    previousTail->next = groupStart;
                }
                break;
            }

            ListNode* nextGroup = groupEnd->next;
            groupEnd->next = nullptr;

            ListNode* reversedHead = reverseList(groupStart);

            if (previousTail == nullptr) {
                answer = reversedHead;
            } else {
                previousTail->next = reversedHead;
            }

            previousTail = groupStart;
            previousTail->next = nextGroup;
            groupStart = nextGroup;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Each complete group is scanned and reversed once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the linked-list nodes supplied by the platform.

The recursive version works on one block at a time. Before changing any links, it walks ahead by `k` nodes. This check is important because a short final block must stay unchanged.

After reversing the first full block, the old head becomes its tail. That tail is connected to the result of processing the rest of the list.

### Small dry run
For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` and `k = 3`, the first call reverses `1, 2, 3` into `3, 2, 1`. The next call sees only `4, 5`, so it returns them unchanged. The final list is `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 5`.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || k <= 1) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode end = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            if (end == null) {
                return head;
            }
            end = end.next;
        }

        ListNode nextPart = reverseKGroup(end, k);
        ListNode previous = nextPart;
        ListNode current = head;

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            ListNode nextNode = current.next;
            current.next = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }

        return previous;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n / k)` for the recursive calls. An empty list, `k = 1`, and a list shorter than `k` all return without changing the links.

A dummy node makes the connections less awkward because `beforeGroup` always points to the node just before the part being reversed. The first full group may change the head, and the dummy handles that automatically.

The old first node of a group becomes the tail after reversal. I move `beforeGroup` to that node before looking for the next group.

Edge cases covered here are an empty list, `k` equal to one, and fewer than `k` nodes at the end. The final short section is left exactly as it was.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || k <= 1) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        ListNode beforeGroup = dummy;

        while (true) {
            ListNode end = beforeGroup;
            for (int i = 0; i < k && end != null; i++) {
                end = end.next;
            }

            if (end == null) {
                break;
            }

            ListNode first = beforeGroup.next;
            ListNode afterGroup = end.next;
            ListNode previous = afterGroup;
            ListNode current = first;

            while (current != afterGroup) {
                ListNode nextNode = current.next;
                current.next = previous;
                previous = current;
                current = nextNode;
            }

            beforeGroup.next = end;
            beforeGroup = first;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. This is an in-place update, so the original list nodes are reused.

